{
  "title": "Trapping Rain Water",
  "description": "`heights[i]` is the height of a block of width 1 standing at position `i`. After it rains, water collects in the dips between taller blocks.\n\nReturn the total number of unit squares of water that stay trapped. Water runs off both ends, so nothing is held to the left of the first block or to the right of the last one.\n\n### Constraints\n\n- `1 <= heights.size() <= 2000`\n- `0 <= heights[i] <= 10000`\n- Inputs are passed as function arguments and the answer is returned from the function. Do not read from standard input or print the result.\n",
  "reference": "int trap(vector<int> heights) {\n    int left = 0, right = (int)heights.size() - 1;\n    int left_max = 0, right_max = 0, water = 0;\n    while (left < right) {\n        if (heights[left] < heights[right]) {\n            left_max = max(left_max, heights[left]);\n            water += left_max - heights[left];\n            ++left;\n        } else {\n            right_max = max(right_max, heights[right]);\n            water += right_max - heights[right];\n            --right;\n        }\n    }\n    return water;\n}\n",
  "template": "int trap(vector<int> heights) {\n    // Return how much water is trapped between the blocks.\n    return 0;\n}\n",
  "tags": [
    "Arrays",
    "Two Pointers",
    "Dynamic Programming",
    "Stack"
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "test_number": 0,
      "explanation": "Water fills the dips: 1 unit at position 2, then 1 + 2 + 1 = 4 units at positions 4 to 6 under the block of height 3, and 1 unit at position 9. The total is 6."
    },
    {
      "test_number": 1,
      "explanation": "The blocks of height 4 and 5 at the ends form a basin whose water level is 4. The blocks inside are 2, 0, 3, and 2 high, so they hold 2 + 4 + 1 + 2 = 9 units."
    }
  ],
